# ORDER MATTERS: the LAST matching pattern wins, so the catch-all comes FIRST and
# specific overrides after it. Verify with `git check-attr text -- <path>`, never by
# reading this file — I first wrote it with the catch-all last and check-attr still
# reported `auto`.
#
# Why this file exists, measured rather than assumed. The Hub registry exporter
# records `bytes` and `sha256` for every data file a dataset card declares, hashing
# WORKING-TREE bytes. With core.autocrlf=true, this box checked out
# tasks/artifacts/corpus_proposals/oapen_open_books/oapen_open_books.jsonl as
# 633553 bytes / 48 CRLF / sha 79fee2dc, while HEAD stores 633505 / 0 CRLF /
# sha 5f2a0c2c. The committed hub_registry.json therefore recorded the Windows
# figures, matched `--check` locally, and was STALE on every Linux CI run.
# A published content hash is only reproducible if the content is byte-identical
# everywhere, so files whose bytes are hashed must never be translated.

# Default: normalise to LF in the repo, check out natively.
* text=auto

# Corpus/telemetry data whose BYTES are hashed and published. Never translate.
*.jsonl  -text
*.ndjson -text

# Generated artifacts that are diffed or hashed byte-for-byte.
*.xml    -text
hub_registry.json  -text
runs_readout.json  -text
training_runs.json -text
